Markup Shorthands: markdown on
Abstract: Allow a suspended coroutine to be cancelled. Cancellation is a
          lightweight marking operation performed while the coroutine is
          suspended; the effect takes place when the coroutine is resumed,
          by invoking `promise.unhandled_cancellation()` followed by
          `co_await promise.final_suspend()`. This provides a third,
          exception-free exit path for coroutines, enabling efficient
          propagation of cancellation across nested coroutine hierarchies
          without throwing and catching an exception at every coroutine
          frame.
</pre>

# Revision history # {#revisions}

## Changes since R0 ## {#since-r0}

- Initial revision. Rebased on [[!N5051]].

# Introduction # {#introduction}

C++20 coroutines are extremely flexible in most respects, with one notable
exception: the ways in which a coroutine can be *stopped* are rigidly fixed.
A coroutine can only be stopped in two ways: by returning a value, and by
throwing an exception. This proposal argues that a third exit path —
*collaborative cancellation* — is missing.

The key insight is that the defining feature of a coroutine is that it can
be *suspended*. A suspended coroutine can be *destroyed*, and a suspended
coroutine can be *resumed*. This proposal observes that a suspended
coroutine can also be *cancelled*:

> We like suspended coroutines. A suspended coroutine already contains
> all of the information needed to destroy it; it therefore also contains
> all of the information needed to cancel it.

The mechanism proposed here is deliberately minimal and, we believe,
self-consistent:

- while a coroutine is suspended, `request_cancel()` marks it as
  *cancelled* — a cheap, non-throwing, non-destroying operation;
- when a cancelled coroutine is subsequently *resumed*, the mark takes
  effect: instead of continuing at the suspend point, the coroutine invokes
  `promise.unhandled_cancellation()` and then
  `co_await promise.final_suspend()`, mirroring what happens when an
  exception escapes the coroutine body.

This design has a number of desirable properties:

- cancellation is a *marking* operation, not an *execution* operation, so
  it can be performed concurrently with the resumer's thread without
  executing any user code;
- the compiler does not need to generate any additional destruction code:
  cancellation reuses the existing suspension and finalization machinery;
- cancellation is composable: it can be converted to an exception where an
  exception is expected, and an exception can be converted to cancellation
  where cancellation is expected;
- nested coroutine hierarchies can propagate cancellation without throwing
  and catching an exception at every coroutine frame.

# Motivation # {#motivation}

## The two exit paths ## {#two-exit-paths}

Currently there are two ways to terminate the execution of a coroutine:

1. *Returning a value.* If a coroutine is cancelled by returning a value
   (for example, by checking `std::stop_token::stop_requested()` and
   returning early), the coroutine's return type typically has to be
   changed to something like `std::expected`, and every
   `await_resume` has to return `std::expected` as well. This is
   invasive, and it has not been widely adopted.

2. *Throwing an exception.* Using exceptions to cancel coroutines is the
   popular approach, and most libraries do it. But this approach has a
   cost: exceptions are expensive. When a child coroutine is cancelled,
   the exception is caught by the child's
   `promise::unhandled_exception()`, stored, and rethrown into the parent
   coroutine. This happens at *every* coroutine frame along the way.

Consider a task hierarchy of depth $N$. Cancelling the innermost task
causes $O(N)$ throw/catch pairs, each of which involves unwinding a
coroutine frame, storing an `exception_ptr`, and rethrowing it in the
parent. For deep hierarchies this cost dominates.

## Cancellation as a third exit path ## {#third-exit-path}

A coroutine can be suspended, and a suspended coroutine can be resumed or
destroyed. This proposal adds a third operation on suspended coroutines:
*cancellation*.

Destruction of a suspended coroutine can be decomposed into two steps:

1. destroying all live local variables of the coroutine body (objects whose
   lifetimes are dynamic), and
2. destroying the coroutine's common state (parameters, coroutine-internal
   state, and the promise object, which live for the whole coroutine).

Cancelling a coroutine is *not* destroying it; cancellation does strictly
less than destruction. There is only one thing it can choose to do: dispose
of the coroutine body's local variables, i.e. step 1.

How does cancellation fit into the existing coroutine design? The key is to
*mimic exceptions*. When a coroutine enters `unhandled_exception()` due to
an exception, the coroutine is *not* destroyed. (If `unhandled_exception()`
itself exits via an exception, the coroutine is destroyed and the exception
propagates; see [CWG2934](https://wg21.link/cwg2934).) Cancelling a coroutine
likewise does not mean destroying it immediately. Entering
`unhandled_exception()` is part of the execution of the coroutine, so
cancelling a coroutine should likewise be part of the execution of the
coroutine.

This leads to an apparent contradiction: a coroutine should be cancellable
only while suspended, but cancellation should take effect during execution.

The resolution is the key design idea of this proposal: **while suspended,
cancelling a coroutine is marking it; when the coroutine is resumed, the
mark takes effect.**

Just as an uncaught exception causes the coroutine to execute
`unhandled_exception()`, a cancelled coroutine executes
`unhandled_cancellation()`. A cancelled coroutine therefore only needs to
destroy its local variables, not its common state.

With this design, all problems are solved. The design is self-consistent
and lightweight: the compiler does not need to generate additional
destruction code; it only needs to execute existing code more flexibly,
based on the cancellation state.

# Design considerations # {#design-considerations}

## Cancellation is a marking operation ## {#marking}

`request_cancel()` has no effect other than setting a flag in the coroutine
state. In particular, it does not:

- destroy the coroutine (unlike `destroy()`);
- resume the coroutine (unlike `resume()`);
- throw an exception;
- execute any user code.

This means `request_cancel()` can be called from any execution agent,
including a different thread, without running arbitrary code in the
coroutine. The only synchronization requirement is on the flag itself.

The flag is observed when the coroutine is resumed: resumption of a
cancelled coroutine does not continue at the suspend point, but instead
enters the cancellation handler. This defuses the "suspended vs. executing"
contradiction described in [[#third-exit-path]].

## Preconditions ## {#preconditions}

`request_cancel()` requires the coroutine to be suspended and not already
cancelled. `cancel_requested()` requires the coroutine to be suspended.

The reason for these preconditions is that the cancellation flag is part of
the coroutine state, which is only stable while the coroutine is suspended.
Resuming or destroying a coroutine concurrently with `request_cancel()`
results in a data race on the coroutine state, which is already undefined
behavior for concurrent resumption.

## The cancellation handler is optional ## {#handler-optional}

Unlike `unhandled_exception()`, `unhandled_cancellation()` is not required
to be defined by every promise type. A coroutine whose promise type does not
define `unhandled_cancellation()` is not *cancellation-aware*; calling
`request_cancel()` on such a coroutine is undefined behavior. This mirrors
the situation of a promise type that does not define
`unhandled_exception()` when exceptions are enabled: the program is
ill-formed only if the coroutine body can actually throw.

[Note: A coroutine that is not cancellation-aware does not carry the
cancellation flag in its coroutine state, so no runtime cost is paid by
coroutines that do not opt in. — end note]

## Relationship with `std::stop_token` ## {#stop-token}

`std::stop_token` is a *cooperative* mechanism: the callee checks
`stop_requested()` at well-defined points and cooperates. This proposal is
complementary: `std::stop_token` answers *whether* cancellation has been
requested; `request_cancel()` provides a cheap way to *deliver* that
request to a suspended coroutine, and `unhandled_cancellation()` provides a
uniform place to *respond* to it.

The two mechanisms compose naturally, e.g.:

```cpp
struct stop_token_awaiter {
    std::stop_token t;
    bool cancelled = false;

    bool await_ready() { return false; }

    template <class Promise>
    void await_suspend(std::coroutine_handle<Promise> handle) {
        if (t.stop_requested()) {
            cancelled = true;
            handle.request_cancel();
        }
    }

    void await_resume() {
        if (cancelled) {
            std::unreachable();
        }
    }
};
```

## Exceptions and cancellation are interconvertible ## {#interconvertible}

Because cancellation is a *state* rather than a control-flow mechanism, it
can be converted to an exception where an exception is expected, and vice
versa. The awaiter examples in [[#examples]] show how to:

- convert an exception produced by a child coroutine into a cancellation of
  the parent (so that intermediate coroutines do not pay the cost of
  throwing and catching);
- convert a cancellation of a child coroutine into an exception in the
  parent (for APIs that must surface cancellation as an exception).

## No new syntax ## {#no-new-syntax}

This proposal introduces no new syntax. The entire feature consists of two
member functions of `std::coroutine_handle` and one new promise member
function, `unhandled_cancellation()`. This makes the feature implementable
by existing compilers with modest effort (see the companion implementation
plan) and adoptable incrementally by libraries.

# Proposal # {#proposal}

## Core language: cancellation handler ## {#core-language}

[Note: The following changes are relative to [[!N5051]].]

A coroutine whose promise type declares a member `unhandled_cancellation`
is a *cancellation-aware* coroutine.

In the replacement body of a cancellation-aware coroutine
([dcl.fct.def.coroutine]), a *cancellation handler* is established as
follows: when the coroutine is resumed and its cancellation state is set,
instead of continuing at the suspend point, the coroutine behaves as if the
following were executed:

```cpp
promise.unhandled_cancellation();
goto final-suspend;     // i.e. co_await promise.final_suspend();
```

where `final-suspend` is the exposition-only label of the final await
expression ([dcl.fct.def.coroutine]).

If `promise.unhandled_cancellation()` exits via an exception, the coroutine
state is destroyed — the promise object, the parameter copies, and the
coroutine-internal state are destroyed, as if by `destroy()` — and the
exception propagates to the caller or resumer.

[Note: The current wording for `unhandled_exception()` says that the
coroutine "is considered suspended at the final suspend point" when
`unhandled_exception()` exits via an exception. This is a known wording
defect, [CWG2934](https://wg21.link/cwg2934): implementations destroy the
promise object, the parameter copies, and the coroutine state before
propagating the exception, and CWG confirmed (2024-11-08) that the
implementations are correct and that the normative wording needs to be
amended. This proposal specifies the corrected semantics for
`unhandled_cancellation()`: an exception escaping from the cancellation
handler destroys the coroutine, exactly as an exception escaping from
`unhandled_exception()` does in practice. — end note]

## Library: `coroutine_handle` ## {#library}

Add to the primary template `std::coroutine_handle<Promise>`:

```cpp
// [coroutine.handle.cancel], cancellation
void request_cancel() const;
bool cancel_requested() const;
```

*Preconditions:* `*this` refers to a suspended coroutine.
For `request_cancel()`: the coroutine has not already been cancelled.

*Effects of `request_cancel()`:* sets the cancellation state of the
coroutine.

*Returns of `cancel_requested()`:* `true` if `request_cancel()` has been
called for the coroutine, otherwise `false`.

The cancellation state is *sticky*: once set, it remains set until the
coroutine is destroyed, mirroring `std::stop_token::stop_requested()`.

A cancellation *takes effect* when the coroutine is resumed: the resumption
does not continue at the suspend point, but instead enters the cancellation
handler described in [[#core-language]].

[Note: `coroutine_handle<void>` does not provide these member functions.
Locating the cancellation state in the coroutine frame requires knowledge
of the promise type's size and alignment; a type-erased handle does not
carry that information. Awaiter code that needs to cancel a coroutine of
unknown promise type can use a template as shown in [[#examples]]. — end note]

## Summary of behavior ## {#summary}

| Operation | Suspended coroutine | Effect |
|---|---|---|
| `resume()` | not cancelled | continues at the suspend point |
| `resume()` | cancelled | executes `unhandled_cancellation()`, then final suspend |
| `destroy()` | any | destroys the coroutine (unchanged) |
| `request_cancel()` | any | sets the cancellation state (no user code runs) |
| `cancel_requested()` | any | queries the cancellation state |

# Examples # {#examples}

## Cancelling when a task cannot be scheduled ## {#example-schedule}

```cpp
template <class Promise>
void await_suspend(std::coroutine_handle<Promise> handle) {
    if (!post_to_queue(handle)) {
        handle.request_cancel();
    }
}
```

If the promise type is known, the handle can be typed as
`std::coroutine_handle<promise>` and the promise can attach more detailed
cancellation information.

## Observing cancellation in the handler ## {#example-observe}

`cancel_requested()` exists so that the promise does not need to store a
separate `bool` for observers to check. A typical use of
`unhandled_cancellation` is to store extra cancellation state inside the
promise, e.g. to convert it to an exception:

```cpp
struct task {
    struct promise_type {
        std::exception_ptr e;
        // ...

        void unhandled_cancellation() {
            if (!std::coroutine_handle<promise>::from_promise(*this).cancel_requested()) {
                std::unreachable();
            }
            e = std::make_exception_ptr(cancelled_exception{});
        }

        void unhandled_exception() {
            e = std::current_exception();
        }
    };
};
```

## Propagating cancellation between coroutines ## {#example-propagate}

To propagate cancellation state between coroutines, an awaiter can be
designed as follows:

```cpp
struct propagate_cancellation_awaiter {
    std::coroutine_handle<promise> child;

    bool await_ready() { return false; }

    void await_suspend(std::coroutine_handle<promise> parent) {
        if (child.cancel_requested()) {
            parent.request_cancel();
        }
        // ...
    }
    void await_resume() {}
};
```

## Avoiding repeated throwing and catching ## {#example-no-rethrow}

The new API can be used to avoid throwing and catching exceptions in every
coroutine frame. Different awaiters can convert exceptions to cancellation,
and cancellation to exceptions:

```cpp
struct exception_to_cancellation_awaiter {
    std::coroutine_handle<promise> child;
    bool await_ready() { return false; }
    void await_suspend(std::coroutine_handle<promise> parent) {
        if (auto &&e = child.promise().get_exception()) {
            parent.promise().set_exception(e);
            parent.request_cancel();
        }
        // ...
    }
    void await_resume() {}
};

struct cancellation_to_exception_awaiter {
    std::coroutine_handle<promise> child;
    std::exception_ptr curr_e;
    bool await_ready() { return false; }
    void await_suspend(std::coroutine_handle<promise> parent) {
        if (auto &&e = child.promise().get_exception(); child.cancel_requested() && !e) {
            curr_e = std::make_exception_ptr(cancelled_exception{});
        } else if (e) {
            curr_e = e;
        }
        // ...
    }
    void await_resume() {
        if (curr_e) {
            std::rethrow_exception(curr_e);
        }
    }
};
```

Only the coroutine that *originates* the exception needs to throw, and only
the coroutine that *handles* the exception needs to catch. Intermediate
coroutines that merely propagate the exception perform no throwing or
catching at all.

## Nested generators ## {#example-generator}

Consider a nested `std::generator` ([[P2505R5]]). Today, when the innermost
generator throws, the exception is stored and rethrown at every nesting
level: `unhandled_exception()` of every intermediate generator captures the
exception, and the recursive awaiter rethrows it into the parent. With this
proposal, the innermost generator stores the exception once, marks the
parent as cancelled, and every intermediate generator merely propagates the
cancellation mark. Only the root generator, at the boundary with the
client, converts the mark back into an exception — a single throw/catch
pair regardless of the nesting depth. See the accompanying benchmark
([[#impact-on-performance]]) for measurements.

# Impact on the standard # {#impact-on-the-standard}

This proposal modifies:

- [dcl.fct.def.coroutine] — coroutine definitions, to specify the
  cancellation handler;
- [coroutine.handle] — class template `coroutine_handle`, to add
  `request_cancel()` and `cancel_requested()`.

No other parts of the standard are affected. In particular, no changes to
the grammar, to `coroutine_traits`, or to any other standard library
component are required.

# Impact on existing code # {#impact-on-existing-code}

No existing code becomes ill-formed. Coroutines whose promise types do not
define `unhandled_cancellation()` are unaffected and do not carry a
cancellation flag. Libraries that wish to support cancellation opt in by:

- defining `unhandled_cancellation()` in their promise types, and
- calling `request_cancel()` / `cancel_requested()` where appropriate.

Because the new member functions are additive, they do not break binary
compatibility of existing coroutine frames (see [[#impact-on-abi]]).

# Impact on performance # {#impact-on-performance}

## Impact on ABI ## {#impact-on-abi}

For coroutines that are not cancellation-aware, there is no change to the
coroutine frame layout and no runtime cost.

For cancellation-aware coroutines, an implementation needs to store one
flag in the coroutine state and check it on resumption. On the Itanium ABI
and the LLVM switched-resume ABI, this can be done without changing the
fixed header offsets (resume/destroy function pointers and promise
projection), by allocating the flag after the header fields. This keeps the
promise projection (`coro.promise`) and the `done()` query ABI-stable.

[Note: The proposed extension point for LLVM is a single marker intrinsic
used only to locate the cancellation entry block, plus front-end builtins for
`request_cancel()` / `cancel_requested()` that lower to the existing promise
projection intrinsics and a flag field; coroutines that do not use the new
facility have byte-identical frame layouts to today. — end note]

## Measured benchmark ## {#benchmark}

To validate the motivation, we compared exception propagation through nested
generators with and without the cancellation chain, using the stock
`std::generator` implementation (one throw/catch pair per nesting level) and
a variant that uses `request_cancel()` / `unhandled_cancellation()` to
propagate the failure. The innermost generator throws after yielding a few
elements; the benchmark measures one round per failing iteration, plus a
non-throwing baseline.

Clang 21, `-O2`, x86-64, rounds = 200,000:

| nesting depth | stock (ns/round overhead) | cancellation (ns/round overhead) | speedup |
|---|---|---|---|
| 3 | 12,096 | 4,655 | 2.6× |
| 5 | 17,590 | 4,900 | 3.6× |

Key observations:

- The stock implementation's overhead grows linearly with the nesting depth
  (≈ 2.7 µs per extra level): each level performs one `rethrow_exception` and
  one catch.
- The cancellation chain's overhead is nearly constant (≈ 0.25 µs per extra
  level): intermediate frames only store a flag and transfer to the parent
  via symmetric transfer, which is a tail call and does not add stack or
  throw/catch cost.
- The normal (non-throwing) path is essentially unaffected (within noise;
  the cancellation-aware coroutines pay one flag load + branch per resume).

The benchmark also confirmed that the cancellation chain must be driven by
symmetric transfer: transferring back to the parent on the failure path lets
the whole chain unwind within a single client-side resume, so no stale
yielded values are exposed to the caller.

## Impact on code size and speed ## {#impact-on-speed}

- `request_cancel()` lowers to a single store of a boolean flag;
- `cancel_requested()` lowers to a single load;
- the resumption check is one load and one branch, executed only for
  cancellation-aware coroutines;
- no destruction code is generated for the cancellation path, because the
  cancellation handler is followed by the existing final suspend.

The cancellation path therefore costs, at most, a few instructions per
coroutine that opts in, and nothing for coroutines that do not.

# Proposed wording # {#proposed-wording}

## Core language ## {#wording-core}

[Editor's note: The following wording is relative to [[!N5051]].]
[Drafting note: wording to be refined with the project editor.]

Modify [dcl.fct.def.coroutine] paragraph 4 (the replacement body) as
follows:

> A coroutine behaves as if the top-level cv-qualifiers in all
> *parameter-declaration*s in the declarator of its *function-definition*
> were removed and its *function-body* were replaced by the following
> *replacement body*:
>
> ```
> {
>   promise-type promise promise-constructor-arguments ;
>   try {
>     co_await promise.initial_suspend() ;
>     function-body
>   } catch ( ... ) {
>     if (!initial-await-resume-called)
>       throw ;
>     promise.unhandled_exception() ;
>   }
>   final-suspend:
>   co_await promise.final_suspend() ;
> }
> ```

Insert a new paragraph after [dcl.fct.def.coroutine] paragraph 4:

> A coroutine whose promise type declares a member function
> `unhandled_cancellation` is *cancellation-aware*. If a cancellation-aware
> coroutine is resumed and its cancellation state is set, the resumption
> does not continue at the suspend point; instead, the coroutine behaves as
> if the following were executed:
>
> ```
> promise.unhandled_cancellation() ;
> goto final-suspend ;
> ```
>
> [Note: The cancellation state is set by
> `request_cancel()` ([coroutine.handle.cancel]). — end note]

Modify [dcl.fct.def.coroutine] paragraph 18:

> If the evaluation of the expression `promise.unhandled_exception()`
> exits via an exception, the coroutine is considered suspended at the
> final suspend point and the exception propagates to the caller or
> resumer.

Add:

> If the evaluation of the expression `promise.unhandled_cancellation()`
> exits via an exception, the coroutine is considered suspended at the
> final suspend point and the exception propagates to the caller or
> resumer.

## Library ## {#wording-library}

Modify [coroutine.handle.general] (class template `coroutine_handle`) as
follows:

> Add to `coroutine_handle<void>`:
>
> ```cpp
> // [coroutine.handle.cancel], cancellation
> void request_cancel() const;
> bool cancel_requested() const;
> ```
>
> Add to `coroutine_handle<Promise>`:
>
> ```cpp
> // [coroutine.handle.cancel], cancellation
> void request_cancel() const;
> bool cancel_requested() const;
> ```

Insert a new subclause [coroutine.handle.cancel], "Cancellation":

> ```
> void request_cancel() const;
> ```
>
> *Preconditions:* `*this` refers to a suspended coroutine. The coroutine
> has not already been cancelled.
>
> *Effects:* Sets the cancellation state of the coroutine.
>
> ```
> bool cancel_requested() const;
> ```
>
> *Preconditions:* `*this` refers to a suspended coroutine.
>
> *Returns:* `true` if the cancellation state of the coroutine is set,
> otherwise `false`.

